right now corporate governance tims they  have a check box or almost compliance  approach what we have is a situation  where they'll be a blue-ribbon panel to  come out and say do you guys really need  to do something say everybody should  separate the CEO and chairman and things  like that we don't think that's very  useful in the governance arena because  the issues the topics the people vary  tremendously from company to company and  our perspective is let's really lay out  the essence of the problem bring in some  research insights and then and then give  the reader a setting to say here's  something had really happened what would  you actually do  where it gets really interesting boards  is something unexpected happens what  would you do if you know kind of one  example would be what would it what  would you do if the CEO unexpectedly  left you know do you really have a plan  in place have you really thought about  it sometimes you know because things are  going along fine you don't really think  carefully about what would happen if in  our book we pick up a variety of topics  that happen on a fairly regular basis  maybe haven't happened to you yet but I  probably likely going to happen what  happens if you have a big accounting  problem or is a big accounting  Restatement what if you get tremendous  push back from the outside about your  pay package you know those are things  that you want to think about ahead of  time as opposed to thinking about them  went when it actually happens one of the  most interesting chapters is on Royal  Dutch Shell that had an accounting  problem a few years ago where they  overstated the amount of oil that they  have in their proven reserves and they  did so by over twenty percent it was a  huge misstatement and the question was  why could something like this happen and  we have some very interesting  information in there where we look at  the structure of the organization  leadership changes cultural changes and  as well as some actual dialogue and and  emails and conversation that took place  among executives and you can see how a  company can potentially have breakdowns  and its internal controls that can lead  to a problem  one of the things we pride ourself on  here at Stanford is we want to base our  recommendations or base our thinking on  real research we tend to take into  account you know what experts in the  field say or what blue ribbon panel say  but all we want to ask the question does  that work or doesn't it so we've tried  to embed that in the book but in a very  specific setting where what would you do  if you were you were confronted with  this kind of request for compensation or  what would you do if you had this kind  of board structure and I think it  provides a really interesting learning  experience in a fairly tight manner but  where it really gets into the essence of  the problem and I think that's what  corporate governance needs to do be  based on research be based on fact and  then also be based on careful thinking
 

